BATCH={'key':'la_kaae','label':'Greater New Orleans: brick forts, a powerhouse and a lost beach','added':'2026-11-18 04:31'}
NPG='https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/'
PLACES=[
# ---- Brick forts of the lake and river approaches ----
N('fort-macomb','Fort Macomb','New Orleans','la','fort','ruins',30.06478,-89.80436,'exact','view','drive','forts','1822–1871',
  'A brick fort on the west shore of Chef Menteur Pass, one of the water routes from Lake Borgne toward New Orleans. The United States built it in 1822, seven years after the British invasion of 1814–15; it was called Fort Wood from 1827 and renamed Fort Macomb in 1851. The barracks burned in 1867 and the Army largely left it after that. Part of the old moat was later made into a marina canal, and boat wakes wore at the outer wall; Hurricane Katrina destroyed the marina in 2005.',
  'A semicircular, bastioned brick fort with casemates: three earth-filled brick bastions, a curving wall facing the pass, and inside the parade ground the barracks, two cisterns and the foundation of the hot-shot furnace that heated cannon balls. West of the fort the old entrance survives as a semicircular brick-walled passage (Register form, 1978).',
  'The sister of Fort Pike that nobody is let into.',
  accn='Owned by the State of Louisiana and closed: the state judged the decaying fort too dangerous for visitors. Do not enter. The Register boundary runs along the highway (US 90) on the west side; whether the walls can be seen from the roadside is not checked on the ground.',
  links=[W('Fort Macomb'),['National Register nomination form (1978)',NPG+'78001429_text']],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('fort-st-john-spanish-fort','Fort St. John (Old Spanish Fort) ruins','New Orleans','la','fort','ruins',30.02222,-90.08333,'site','public','walk','forts,amusements','1808 ruin; amusement park c.1880s–1900s',
  'A brick fort at the mouth of Bayou St. John, guarding the short water route from Lake Pontchartrain into the city. The French first fortified the spot; Wikipedia says the Spanish then built a larger brick fort, San Juan del Bayou, while the National Register form dates most of what stands to 1808, after the American purchase, so the builder of the present walls is not settled. The land later passed to private owners and became a resort and amusement park known as Spanish Fort; an iron submarine now in the Louisiana State Museum was shown here from about 1880 to 1908.',
  'Low brick walls on the west bank of the bayou, just back from Allen Toussaint Boulevard beside the Floral Park part of Lake Vista. The form (1983) says about 90 percent of the fort wall survives, part of it buried under the bayou levee, with brick steps added when the site was a resort garden.',
  'A colonial fort that became the city\'s lakefront playground.',
  accn='City of New Orleans land (owner on the 1983 Register form). Open grass along the bayou; no posted hours were found (not checked). Park in the Floral Park lot to the west.',
  links=[W('Spanish Fort (New Orleans)'),['National Register nomination form (1983)',NPG+'83000530_text']],conf='mixed',
  th=[30.02403,-90.08951],thn='Floral Park lot, Lake Vista',brick='yes'),
N('fort-proctor','Fort Proctor (Beauregard\'s Castle)','Shell Beach','la','fort','ruins',29.86731,-89.67828,'exact','view','boat','forts','1856–1860',
  'A square, two-storey brick tower begun in 1856 on the shore of Lake Borgne to guard the water route toward New Orleans; P. G. T. Beauregard supervised the work, hence its other names. Storm damage and then the Civil War stopped it, and it was never finished or garrisoned. The Mississippi River-Gulf Outlet canal of the 1960s cut off all land access, and the lake has since surrounded it.',
  'The tower stands in water about a foot deep. The Register form (1978) describes a spreading brick base with cisterns below, sixteen brick piers, outer walls of Flemish-bond brick about four feet thick, and granite lintels and sills; the vaults for the gun roof were never built.',
  'A castle-like tower sinking into Lake Borgne.',
  accn='Private property (FortWiki; the 1978 form names a private company as owner). It can be seen in the distance from Shell Beach, at the end of LA 46; reaching it takes a boat or kayak (Atlas Obscura puts it at about a twenty-minute paddle). Whether landing is allowed is not known: ask first. Do not climb on the walls.',
  links=[W('Fort Proctor'),['National Register nomination form (1978)',NPG+'78003067_text'],['FortWiki: Fort Proctor','https://fortwiki.com/Fort_Proctor'],['Garden & Gun: Fortress of Solitude in Louisiana','https://gardenandgun.com/fortress-of-solitude-in-louisiana']],brick='?'),
N('battery-bienvenue','Battery Bienvenue','Chalmette','la','fort','ruins',29.98528,-89.88111,'site','view','boat','forts','1815 or later–1872',
  'A coastal gun battery at the fork of Bayou Bienvenue and Bayou Villeré, on the route the British used to approach New Orleans in late 1814. It was about 600 feet wide, with its guns facing Lake Borgne and a moat joined to the bayou, and it was given up in 1872. When it was built is not settled: FortWiki dates it from 1815, Richard Campanella writes that army engineers built it in 1835, and a 1979 arms-collectors\' article gives 1826.',
  'Ruins in open marsh. Wikipedia and FortWiki say the remains still stand; what is left of the walls was not confirmed from a source opened here, and FortWiki notes the guns cannot be made out on satellite pictures.',
  '',
  accn='Reached only by boat (FortWiki, which also warns of snakes). Who owns the ground is not known: view from the water and ask before landing.',
  links=[W('Battery Bienvenue'),['FortWiki: Battery Bienvenue','https://fortwiki.com/Fort_Bienvenue_(Louisiana)'],['Richard Campanella: Third System forts (2020)','https://richcampanella.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Advocate_Geographies_2020_03_Third-System-Forts.pdf']],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('tower-dupre','Tower Dupré (Martello Castle) remains','St. Bernard','la','fort','traces',29.94515,-89.83538,'site','ask','boat','forts','1827–1883; destroyed 2005',
  'A two-storey, six-sided tower built to guard the mouth of Bayou Dupré on Lake Borgne. FortWiki says it was begun in 1827, finished in 1830 and given up in 1883; Campanella dates it to 1840. By 1979 it was in private hands as a fishing camp and stood in the water as the shore wore back. Hurricane Katrina knocked it down in 2005.',
  'A small island of broken masonry and piles of brick in the lake (a visitor\'s account, 2015).',
  '',
  accn='Private (FortWiki). Boat only; ask first. Nothing stands.',
  links=[['FortWiki: Tower Dupre','https://fortwiki.com/Tower_Dupre'],['NOLA Forts blog: Tower Dupre (2015)','https://nolaforts.blogspot.com/2015/04/tower-dupre-1828-cp-no-34-n29-56709-w89.html'],['Richard Campanella: Third System forts (2020)','https://richcampanella.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Advocate_Geographies_2020_03_Third-System-Forts.pdf']],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('fort-jackson-la','Fort Jackson','Buras','la','fort','standing',29.3569,-89.4556,'exact','public','drive','forts','1822–1832; surplus 1927',
  'A star-shaped masonry fort on the west bank of the Mississippi about forty miles above its mouth, built between 1822 and 1832. In April 1862 it and Fort St. Philip across the river fought the Union fleet in the battle for control of the river below New Orleans. On December 9, 1863 Black soldiers of the 4th Corps d\'Afrique rose against their officers here; shots were fired but nobody was killed. The Army declared it surplus in 1927, and it now belongs to Plaquemines Parish. Hurricane Katrina put it under floodwater for weeks in 2005, damaging the brick walls and foundations.',
  'Brick walls about 25 feet high around a moat, seen from the park around them; Battery Ransom, a later concrete battery, stands nearby behind low fences (FortWiki, 2021).',
  '',
  accn='Plaquemines Parish owns the fort and the park around it, which write-ups describe as open. The fort itself is closed: FortWiki reports the parish saying it is not structurally sound, though a visitor in November 2021 found the sally port open. The Fort Jackson Museum is about a mile south at 38039 Highway 23, Buras; its hours are given differently by different write-ups (weekdays 8 to 4, or daily 9 to 4) and the parish page could not be opened: hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Fort Jackson, Louisiana'),['FortWiki: Fort Jackson','https://www.fortwiki.com/Fort_Jackson_(Louisiana)'],['Plaquemines Parish: Fort Jackson Museum','https://www.plaqueminesparish.com/272/Fort-Jackson-Museum'],['64 Parishes: Jewels of Plaquemines Parish','https://www.64parishes.org/jewels-of-plaquemines-parish']],conf='mixed',
  th=[29.34765,-89.46420],thn='Fort Jackson Museum, 38039 Highway 23',brick='?'),
N('fort-st-philip','Fort St. Philip','Triumph','la','fort','ruins',29.36415,-89.46292,'site','ask','boat','forts','18th century–1898',
  'A masonry fort on the east bank of the Mississippi opposite Fort Jackson, on a site first fortified in the colonial period. It held out through ten-day naval sieges in the War of 1812 and the Civil War. From 1978 to 1989 a small spiritual community lived in the buildings left from its 1898 re-fortification. Hurricanes Katrina and Rita damaged it badly in 2005.',
  'According to the National Park Service as quoted by Wikipedia, the owner reported that only the original brick fort and the concrete batteries of the Spanish-American War remain. Not seen from a public place in any source read here.',
  '',
  accn='Privately owned and badly deteriorated. Reachable only by boat or helicopter, and it floods when the river is high. Ask the owner first; there is no public access.',
  links=[W('Fort St. Philip')],brick='?'),
# ---- Industry ----
N('market-street-power-plant','Market Street Power Plant','New Orleans','la','industry','standing',29.93,-90.06417,'site','view','walk','','c.1901–1973',
  'A riverside power plant just upriver of the Crescent City Connection that once lit much of the city. Wikipedia dates it to 1905; the Preservation Resource Center says the first building went up about 1901. It last made power in 1973 for New Orleans Public Service. Entergy sold it in 2007, a planned residential and entertainment scheme failed and it went in foreclosure in 2015, and in 2022 a group of developers bought in with plans for shops, a hotel and offices.',
  'A huge, boarded-up plant with twin smokestacks over the river (Preservation Resource Center). The building is contaminated with asbestos.',
  '',
  accn='Private and closed; the developers have sealed it, and police answer calls about trespassers. See it from the public streets around it. Not open.',
  links=[W('Market Street Power Plant'),['Preservation Resource Center: Market Street Power Plant tour','https://prcno.org/?p=103578']],conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('michoud-sugar-mill-chimneys','Michoud sugar mill chimneys','New Orleans','la','industry','ruins',30.02196,-89.92207,'area','view','drive','','1827–20th century',
  'Antoine Michoud bought this land in 1827 and ran a sugar plantation and refinery here until his death (NASA says 1863; New Orleans Magazine says 1862). His heirs kept the refinery going into the 20th century. The government bought the land in 1940 and built a factory, which NASA took over in 1961 to build Saturn rocket stages.',
  'Two brick chimneys from the sugar mill stand in front of the long brick factory building facing Old Gentilly Road (SAH Archipedia; NASA).',
  'A sugar mill\'s chimneys outside America\'s rocket factory.',
  accn='Inside NASA\'s Michoud Assembly Facility, which is not open to the public. Seen from Old Gentilly Road only; do not stop at the gate.',
  links=[['NASA: History of Michoud Assembly Facility','https://www.nasa.gov/michoud-history'],['SAH Archipedia: NASA Michoud Assembly Facility','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/81387'],['New Orleans Magazine: From Muskrats to Moonships','https://www.myneworleans.com/from-muskrats-to-moonships/']],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('ford-assembly-plant-arabi','Ford Motor Company Assembly Plant','Arabi','la','industry','standing',29.94721,-90.00405,'site','view','drive','','1923–2005',
  'Albert Kahn designed this riverside plant at 7200 North Peters Street, finished in 1923 as one of Ford\'s branch assembly plants. It built cars until 1933, setting a record of 300 a day in 1929, and was then a parts depot. Later it was sold to the War Department, used to clean and repair imported Toyotas, and from 1977 held freight. It flooded after Hurricane Katrina and stood empty for about ten years; when it was listed on the National Register in 2018 it was leased for film-industry storage.',
  'A two-storey concrete and steel plant with walls of steel-sash windows, red brick pilasters and red brick under the windows, the original water tower on the roof, and a small brick oil pump house about 70 yards north (Register form).',
  '',
  accn='Private and not open to the public (National Park Service). See it from North Peters Street.',
  links=[['National Park Service: Ford Motor Company Assembly Plant Louisiana','https://www.nps.gov/places/ford-motor-company-assembly-plant-louisiana.htm'],['National Register nomination form (2018)',NPG+'100002589_text']],brick='some'),
# ---- A lost beach ----
N('lincoln-beach-new-orleans','Lincoln Beach','New Orleans','la','amuse','ruins',30.07106,-89.95153,'site','view','drive','amusements','late 1930s–1964',
  'A 15-acre beach on Lake Pontchartrain opened in the late 1930s for Black New Orleanians, who were kept out of the whites-only Pontchartrain Beach. It had an amusement park and a pool. It closed in 1964, soon after Pontchartrain Beach was integrated, and has been closed ever since. It was listed on the National Register in July 2024, and the city is rebuilding it as a public park.',
  'The city\'s own list of repairs (2023) names a tunnel under the levee, east and west shelters and a brick perimeter wall. Construction was due to start in December 2025; the city now says the beach will reopen in 2027.',
  'The beach Jim Crow built, coming back.',
  accn='City of New Orleans. Closed while it is rebuilt: the city has warned that it is unsafe and that going in is illegal. It lies on the lake side of the levee near Hayne Boulevard and Vincent Road; whether anything can be seen from a public place is not checked.',
  links=[['City of New Orleans: Lincoln Beach redevelopment','https://nola.gov/next/sustainability/lincoln-beach-redevelopment/phase-1-redevelopment/'],['City of New Orleans: Lincoln Beach newsletter, June 2025','https://nola.gov/next/public-works/news/2025/2025/june-2025/lincoln-beach-newsletter/'],['City of New Orleans: Lincoln Beach warning (2023)','https://nola.gov/next/mayors-office/news/articles/june-2023/2023-06-23-lincoln-beach-warning'],['Axios New Orleans: reopening delayed to 2027','https://www.axios.com/local/new-orleans/2025/10/27/lincoln-beach-groundbreaking-delayed-timeline'],['Axios New Orleans: Lincoln Beach on the National Register','https://www.axios.com/local/new-orleans/2024/07/24/lincoln-beach-reopen-2025-national-register-historic-places']],brick='?'),
# ---- Standing places ----
N('old-us-mint-new-orleans','Old U.S. Mint','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.96149,-90.05775,'exact','public','walk','','1835–1909 or 1911',
  'A Greek Revival branch mint designed by William Strickland and built of red brick between 1835 and 1838. The state seized it in 1861 and for a short time it struck Confederate coins. It reopened as an assay office in 1876 and made coins again from 1877, mostly silver dollars. Its working years ended in 1909 by the museum\'s reckoning (the Register list says it was decommissioned in 1911). It has been a branch of the Louisiana State Museum since 1981; Hurricane Katrina damaged the roof, and it reopened in October 2007.',
  'The museum (now the New Orleans Jazz Museum) has a coin exhibit in the basement, where the boilers once stood in a brick cage. Behind the building the foundation of the demolished smokestack can still be seen (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  accn='Louisiana State Museum, 400 Esplanade Avenue. Open Tuesday to Sunday, closed Monday; adults $11 (the Jazz Museum\'s site as quoted in search results, which give closing times of 4 pm and, on Tuesdays, 6 pm). The state museum\'s own page could not be opened: confirm before going.',
  links=[W('New Orleans Mint'),['New Orleans Jazz Museum: visit','https://nolajazzmuseum.org/visit']],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('jackson-barracks-powder-magazine','Jackson Barracks powder magazine','New Orleans','la','fort','standing',29.95987,-90.00637,'site','public','drive','forts','1837',
  'An army post beside the river, paid for by an act of 1832 and first manned in February 1837 and renamed Jackson Barracks in 1866. After the First World War the Army left and the state took it over as headquarters of the Louisiana National Guard. Its 1837 powder magazine became the Guard\'s military museum in 1977. Katrina\'s surge nearly destroyed the post in 2005; it was rebuilt and the museum reopened in a new building beside the magazine.',
  'The 1837 powder magazine stands next to the Jackson Barracks Military Museum (Louisiana National Guard Museums).',
  '',
  accn='Louisiana National Guard, Jackson Barracks Area C, 4209 Chenault Boulevard. Museum hours Tuesday to Friday 9 to 5 and the last Saturday of the month 9 to 2 (the museums\' site as quoted in search results). Jackson Barracks is a working Guard post: what a visitor needs at the gate is not checked.',
  links=[W('Jackson Barracks'),['Louisiana National Guard Museums','https://geauxguardmuseums.com/the-lanationalguardmuseums/about-the-museums/'],['New Orleans Historical: Jackson Barracks museum complex','https://neworleanshistorical.org/items/show/758']],brick='?'),
N('kenilworth-plantation-house','Kenilworth Plantation House','St. Bernard','la','landmark','standing',29.86767,-89.81381,'site','view','drive','','c.1820',
  'A French Creole plantation house facing Bayou Terre aux Boeufs, dated to about 1820 from a conveyance of 1824 that mentions "a dwelling house entirely new". Some of its land goes back to grants to Los Isleños, the Canary Islanders who settled lower St. Bernard; its best-known owner was the politician Albert Estopinal Jr.',
  'The living floor stands on a high brick basement under a rare broken-pitch Creole roof with deep galleries; the walls are heavy timber framing filled with brick (Register form, 2006).',
  '',
  accn='A private home at 2931 Bayou Road. View from the road only.',
  links=[['National Register nomination form (2006)',NPG+'06000317_text']],brick='?'),
]
